{"request": {"input_type": "document", "model": "voyage-4", "text": "The VP of sales explains how email communication with large industrial enterprise customers should be formal, covering greeting and signature conventions, the only written response-time commitment (failure alerts within 15 minutes for the Predictive Maintenance contract), and the rule that prices and discounts never appear in the email body and belong only in an attached formal quote.\n\nInterviewer: Your customers are large industrial groups. How does that shape your emails?\n\nVP of sales: Completely. Enterprise customers get formal emails. Full names and titles in the greeting, no emojis, no exclamation marks, and a proper signature with the contract reference. Their procurement teams read everything, and a casual email looks like a small supplier.\n\nInterviewer: What do your customers expect you to commit to?\n\nVP of sales: Response times, above all. For the Predictive Maintenance contract we promise failure alerts within 15 minutes, and that is the only response time we put in writing. Never invent a faster one to win a deal. If a plant asks for five minutes, the answer goes through the contract, not through an email.\n\nInterviewer: How do you handle pricing in emails?\n\nVP of sales: We never put prices in the body of an email to a customer, whatever their size. Prices go in a formal quote document, attached, with a reference number, because procurement needs that document to open a purchase order. The email says the quote is attached and when it expires.\n\nInterviewer: And discounts?\n\nVP of sales: Same thing, even stricter. No discount appears in writing outside the quote. Our deals are six figures, a percentage in an email is a liability."}, "vector": [0.01613093912601471, -0.04204954206943512, -0.019715601578354836, -0.01462878193706274, -0.009292315691709518, 0.005841172765940428, -0.05720260739326477, -0.05098384618759155, -0.010494457557797432, 0.03002418577671051, -0.001150892348